ry></address></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>OTSUJI TAKAMASA</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>KOMATSU ZENOAH, CO.</name></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>MIYAZAKI, HIROSHI</name></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>OTSUJI, TAKAMASA</name></addressbook></applicant></applicants><inventors><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>MIYAZAKI HIROSHI</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>OTSUJI TAKAMASA</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>MIYAZAKI, HIROSHI</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>OTSUJI, TAKAMASA</name></addressbook></inventor></inventors></parties></bibliographic-data><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="EN"><p>A stratified scavenging two-stroke cycle engine which is increased in leading air sucking efficiency, simple in construction, and low in cost, comprising an exhaust port (22) and a scavenging port (21) connected to a cylinder chamber (10), a leading air sucking port (24) not connected to the cylinder chamber (10) and a crankcase (11), a mixture sucking port (23) connected to the crankcase (11), a scavenging flow path (20) connecting the scavenging port (21) to the crankcase (11), and a piston groove (25) which, in a suction stroke, connects the leading air sucking port (24) to the scavenging port (21) and does not connect the mixture sucking port (23) to the scavenging port (21) and provided in the outer peripheral part of a piston (4), wherein the leading air sucking port (24) is positioned on the opposite side of the mixture sucking port (23) relative to the axis of a cylinder (3).</p></abstract><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="FR"><p>L'invention concerne un moteur à deux temps de balayage à charges stratifiées dont l'efficacité d'aspiration de l'air d'attaque est améliorée. Ce moteur à deux temps, de construction simple et bon marché, comporte un orifice d'échappement (22) et un orifice de balayage (21) reliés à une chambre du cylindre (10), un orifice d'aspiration de l'air d'attaque (24) non relié à la chambre du cylindre (10) et un carter (11), un orifice d'aspiration des mélanges (23) relié au carter (11), un circuit d'écoulement de balayage (20) reliant l'orifice de balayage (21) au carter (11), et une gorge de piston (25) ménagée dans la partie périphérique extérieure d'un piston (4) et qui, dans une course d'aspiration, relie l'orifice d'aspiration de l'air d'attaque (24) à l'orifice de balayage (21) mais ne relie pas l'orifice d'aspiration des mélanges (23) à l'orifice de balayage (21). L'orifice d'aspiration de l'air d'attaque (24) est placé sur le côté opposé de l'orifice d'aspiration des mélanges (23) relativement à l'axe d'un cylindre (3).</p></abstract><copyright>User acknowledges that the Information Retrieval Facility (IRF) and its third party providers retain all right, title and interest in and to this xml under applicable copyright laws. User acquires no ownership rights to this xml including but not limited to its format. User hereby accepts the terms and conditions of the Licence Agreement set forth at http://www.ir-facility.org/legal/marec/data_licence</copyright></patent-document>